● Two things up front, because they explain exactly what you're seeing:

  1. You're currently on main, which is the old flat static site — that's the
     main reason the directory doesn't look like Minimal Mistakes. The migrated
     structure lives on the jekyll-minimal-mistakes branch (commit 8192176).
  2. Even on the migrated branch, the directory is intentionally sparse. Because
     the theme is pulled in via remote_theme, the theme's own folders
     (_layouts/, _includes/, _sass/, assets/) are not in your repo — they're
     fetched from the theme at build time. Your repo only needs _config.yml,
     Gemfile, _data/, and your content. You only add an override file (like
     _includes/head/custom.html) when you want to customize something.

  So a "normal" Minimal Mistakes directory isn't the goal — a minimal one is.

  Adding a new page

  Where: drop a .md (or .html) file at the repository root, or in a subfolder.
  The filename becomes the URL.

  The template (this is what you copy each time):

  ---
  title: "Page Title"
  description: "One-sentence summary shown in search results and social
  previews."
  permalink: /about/
  ---

  Your content here, written in **Markdown**.

  The two fields you asked about:

  ┌─────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │    Field    │                            Role                            │
  ├─────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ title       │ the headline — rendered as the page's <h1> and the         │
  │             │ browser-tab title                                          │
  ├─────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ description │ the summary — used as the meta/Open Graph description      │
  ├─────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ permalink   │ (optional) the URL; omit it and the file path is used      │
  │             │ (about.md → /about.html)                                   │
  └─────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  You do not need to write layout: — I set layout: single (plus author_profile: 
  false, comments: false, share: false, classes: wide) as global defaults in
  _config.yml. So a new page only needs title, plus description/permalink if you
  want them. If you ever want a different look, override with layout: splash
  (big hero) or layout: archive.

  If the page needs custom HTML/JS/CSS (like the existing shell pages), use an
  .html file with the same front matter, then raw HTML:

  ---
  title: "My Interactive Page"
  description: "Short summary."
  permalink: /my-page/
  ---

  <h2>A heading</h2>
  <p>Some content.</p>
  <style>/* scoped styles */</style>
  <script>/* page script */</script>

  To make it show up in the top navigation, add it to _data/navigation.yml:

  main:
    - title: "About"
      url: /about/
    # ...existing entries

  Two special cases (not normal "pages")

  - New visualization demo (a self-contained canvas/WebGL .html): do not add
    front matter. Drop the file at the root and add its path to
    _data/visualizations.yml. It stays a static file and automatically appears
    in the gallery, search, and list pages.
  - Dated blog post: create _posts/YYYY-MM-DD-slug.md. This site isn't set up as
    a blog, but the theme supports it if you want it later.

